concepts.body:
A reference **borrows** a value without taking ownership. `&x` creates a shared reference: you can read through it, and the owner keeps the value.

```rust
fn count(ledger: &Vec<String>) -> usize {
    ledger.len()
}

let ledger = vec![String::from("gold")];
let n = count(&ledger); // lend it
println!("{n} {ledger:?}"); // still ours
```

You can have as many shared references at once as you like. A `&T` is `Copy`: handing out a borrow never moves anything. While a shared borrow is alive, the owner can't change the value. The value is **frozen**.

`&mut x` lends the value with permission to change it. The variable itself must be declared `mut`.

```rust
fn stamp(entry: &mut String) {
    entry.push_str(" [checked]");
}

let mut entry = String::from("rum");
stamp(&mut entry);
println!("{entry}"); // rum [checked]
```

Through a `&mut` you can read, write, and call `&mut self` methods like `push`. You use `*r` to assign through it when the type is a plain value: `*count += 1;`.

A borrow lasts until the reference is **used for the last time**, not until the end of the block. This is called *non-lexical lifetimes* (NLL).

```rust
let mut gold = 10;
let peek = &gold;
println!("{peek}");    // last use of peek: the shared borrow ends here
let grab = &mut gold;  // fine
*grab += 5;
```

So reordering code often fixes E0502 or E0499: do all the reading, then all the writing.

A reference must never outlive the value it points to. Returning a reference to a local is rejected (**E0515**), because the local is dropped when the function returns:

```rust
fn title(name: &str) -> &str {
    let t = format!("Captain {name}");
    &t // error[E0515]: return the owned String instead
}
```

When a function returns a reference and takes several, rustc can't guess which input it borrows from (**E0106**). You name it with a **lifetime parameter**:

```rust
fn longest<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str {
    if b.len() > a.len() { b } else { a }
}
```

Read `'a` as "the result lives no longer than both inputs". Annotations don't change how long anything lives. They describe the relationship so the compiler can check every caller. Tie the result only to the inputs it can actually come from.

